{"text": "12/20/2015 Legal-Services Unit Draws Fire for Its Migrant Workers - WSJ\n\nTHE WALL STREET JOURNAL.\n\nThis copy is for your personal, non-commercial use only. To order presentation-ready copies for distribution to your colleagues, clients or customers visit\nhttp://www.djreprints.com.\n\nhttp://www.wsj.com/articles/SB919194255375377500\n\nSOUTH EAST JOURNAL\nLegal-Services Unit Draws Fire for Its\nMigrant Workers\n\nBy MOTOKO RICH Staff Reporter of The Wall Street Journal\nUpdated Feb. 17, 1999 12:01 a.m. ET\n\nRALEIGH -- Affixed to the top of Mary Lee Hall\u2019s computer is a red bumper sticker that\nreads in Spanish: \u201dSi quieres paz, lucha por la justicia.\u201d Translation: \"If you want peace,\nfight for justice.\u201d\n\nLately, though, Ms. Hall, a federally funded lawyer who has represented migrant farm\nworkers for 22 years, has found it difficult to achieve either.\n\nMs. Hall and her colleagues, attorneys at a unit of Legal Services of North Carolina\ndedicated to detecting violations of farm workers\u2019 rights, now are spending much of\ntheir time fending off attacks that they themselves violated the law. Their alleged\nbreach: illegally recruiting foreign workers, mostly from Mexico, to sue North Carolina\nfarmers.\n\nAt issue is a two-week trip Ms. Hall and two other attorneys took to 12 rural Mexican\nvillages in early 1998. According to Ms. Hall, the trip was designed to educate workers\nwho had come to the U.S. on temporary visas about their legal rights. In addition, the\nattorneys planned to contact existing clients who had returned to Mexico. But critics\nsay the lawyers also drummed up new clients -- violating federal law and misusing the\nfederal funds for the lawyers\u2019 program.\n\nExhibit A in the critics\u2019 case is a videotape of Ms. Hall talking to Mexicans in one village.\nWhen news of the tape found its way to Congress last February, it ignited a political\nfirestorm that is still raging. Ms. Hall\u2019s program was ordered to pay back the $17,050\ncost of the trip and was dissolved into an umbrella group that closely monitors the\nlawyers\u2019 work. Now farm-worker-rights lawyers from Florida, Texas and elsewhere are\n\nhttp://www.wsj.com/articles/SB919194255375377500 W/7",
